    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p3-q12-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Suppose the equation $ax^2+bx+c=0$ has three different solutions. Prove that $a=b=c=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a=b=c=0$.\n\nSubtract equations for distinct roots and divide by their nonzero differences. Subtracting the resulting equations gives $a=0$; substitution then gives $b=c=0$.",
          "worked": "Let the three distinct solutions be $x_1,x_2,x_3$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}ax_1^2+bx_1+c&=0\\\\ax_2^2+bx_2+c&=0\\\\ax_3^2+bx_3+c&=0\\end{align*}$$\nSubtract the second equation from the first:\n$$\\begin{align*}a(x_1^2-x_2^2)+b(x_1-x_2)&=0\\\\(x_1-x_2)\\bigl(a(x_1+x_2)+b\\bigr)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nSince $x_1\\ne x_2$,\n$$a(x_1+x_2)+b=0.$$\nSimilarly, subtracting the third equation from the second gives\n$$a(x_2+x_3)+b=0.$$\nSubtract these last two equations:\n$$a(x_1-x_3)=0.$$\nSince $x_1\\ne x_3$, $a=0$. Substituting into $a(x_1+x_2)+b=0$ gives $b=0$. Finally, substituting into $ax_1^2+bx_1+c=0$ gives $c=0$. Therefore $a=b=c=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 124
      },
      {
        "id": "p3-q12-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p3-q12-b-stem",
